  "hero.kicker": "Long essay · Computing · Machine learning",
  "hero.title": "What It Takes to Build and Run a Frontier Large Language Model",
  "hero.sub": "A small model you can take apart in your browser, plus a bill that runs into tens of millions of dollars.",
  "hero.lead": "<a href=\"../how-a-chatbot-writes/\">“How a Chatbot Writes a Sentence”</a> covered the basic moves: tokens, attention, drawing words by probability, and training. This essay goes on from there: what each layer of a real Transformer is actually computing; how many parameters it has and how many operations each token costs; given a budget of compute, how big the model should be and how much text it should read; how many chips, days, megawatt-hours and dollars one training run takes; why a model is slow and expensive once it is running, and which tricks engineers use to make it faster and cheaper; and finally, where the frontier stood in November 2026. After reading, you should be able to follow the passages on compute in a model technical report or an earnings call, know which few numbers matter most, and see what limits them.",
  "hero.note": "<strong>A note up front:</strong> this essay was written by AI (Claude), and it is about things of the same kind as me. The small model on the page is real: a Transformer with 27,930 parameters, trained with the scripts in this directory. Its weights download with the page, and every time you drag a slider it really recomputes in your browser. The large-model parts use formulas from published papers and public hardware specifications. The text says, each time, which numbers the model computed, which the companies published, and which are third-party estimates. There is no investment advice here, and no forecasts of my own.",

